Subject: build with bionic: header clashes, rthdr0 addresses, no fopencookie

Why:
- bionic's <netinet/in.h> includes <linux/ipv6.h>, so nft's own struct ipv6hdr is a redefinition;
  use the layout-identical kernel struct.
- bionic's <netinet/ip.h> already defines ip_auth_hdr, ip_esp_hdr, ip_comp_hdr (same layout), and
  <netinet/tcp.h> includes <linux/tcp.h> whose TCP_FLAG_* enumerators clash with nft's
  (src/ipopt.c includes both); include them first and rename nft's enumerators via macros.
- bionic's <netinet/ip6.h> (from OpenBSD) has no ip6r0_addr[] in struct ip6_rthdr0.
- bionic has no fopencookie() at all (neither in the NDK r30 headers nor in libc.a); use the BSD
  funopen() that bionic does provide.
- bionic's <netinet/igmp.h> lacks IGMP_V1/V2_MEMBERSHIP_REPORT and IGMP_V2_LEAVE_GROUP (added
  with #ifndef, values from RFC 1112/2236 as in glibc).
All other changes are under __ANDROID__ only; byte offsets used by nft payload templates are unchanged.
Source: written for this module. No Android/bionic build of nftables exists in AOSP or
termux-packages (see ndk/REFERENCES.md); the funopen() substitution is the usual BSD/bionic
replacement for fopencookie() on BSD-derived libcs (bionic <stdio.h> declares funopen()).
Compared with github.com/der-exp/android_external_nftables commit b1520ae2 (an AOSP-tree port
found by the survey), which works around the same header clashes and also uses funopen().


